[mytestmodule.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/19617665/mytestmodule.zip)

### Tested versions

Reproducible in 4.4 and 4.4.1 (those are the versions I've tried).

### System information

Windows 10 - 4.4.1 stable - compiled from source with D3D12 enabled, compiler VS2022

### Issue description

You can not use custom classes with TypedDictionary inside C++ and expose them to GODOT. The moment you do, your code will not compile.

How I expect it to work:
To compile successfully with custom types such as:
`TypedDictionary<StringName, Ref<MyTemplate>> my_dictionary`

### Steps to reproduce

1. Compile the engine from source
2. Create 2 classes and register both of them with the required macros, include the class you will be using as a custom template inside your TypedDictionary.
```
(class MyClass: public Resource {
GDCLASS(MyClass, Resource);
```
Make sure to register your class inside register_types:

```
void initialize_gas_module(ModuleInitializationLevel p_level) {
	if (p_level != MODULE_INITIALIZATION_LEVEL_SCENE) {
		return;
	}
	// Register classes here
ClassDB::register_class<MyClass>();
ClassDB::register_class<MyCustomTemplate>();
}
```
And do not forget to add your class.cpp files inside your SCsub file of the module!

```
Import('env')
Import("env_modules")

env_sum = env_modules.Clone()

sources = [
    "my_class.cpp",
    "my_template.cpp"
]
env_sum.add_source_files(env.modules_sources, sources) # Add all cpp files to the build
```
3. And do the same with MyCustomTemplate...maybe put a variable or 2 in there:

```
class MyCustomTemplate: public Resource {
GDCLASS(MyCustomTemplate, Resource);

public:
// getters and setters

private:
     float apples;
     float organge;
      int bananas;
}
```
4. Try this:

```
#include "MyTemplate.h"
class MyClass: public Resource {
GDCLASS(MyClass, Resource);

private:
TypedDictionary<StringName, float> my_dictionary //<----- compiles successfully
TypedDictionary<StringName, Ref<MyCustomTemplate>> my_custom_dictionary //<--- this causes compilation error !!!
}
```

